Subject: [PATCH] Ship it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Ship the board: one binary, its environment, and a container to put it
in.

- One go build is the whole board. The generated code carries the
  templates and static files (Tailwind's output too) with //go:embed,
  and main imports the database driver. Outside the binary are only the
  database and the attachments' media/
- The binary migrates its database as it starts. main carries the
  migrations and applies them before OpenDB:

      //go:embed migrations
      var migrations embed.FS

      applied, err := maago.Migrate(ctx, maago.DBConfig{}, migrations)

  It applies what the database has not, with the record and the checks
  of maago migrate: a file changed since it was applied, or a migration
  the database has and the binary does not, stops it. Two copies that
  start at once apply each file once; the second waits. -createsuperuser
  runs after it too, so it works on an empty database
- It serves with maago.Serve(ctx, ":"+port, h). On SIGTERM, as a
  platform stops a container, it takes no new connections and waits up
  to 30s for the requests running (what they gave maago.AfterCommit
  included); a second signal ends it at once
- It listens on PORT (8000 without it), the name hosts and containers
  give it
- Everything else comes from the environment too: MAAGO_DATABASE_URL,
  BOARD_SITE_URL, BOARD_WEBHOOK_URL and BOARD_WEBHOOK_TOKEN.
  BOARD_SITE_URL is required now: without it the server stops at once
  with

      BOARD_SITE_URL is not set: set it to the site's address, https://board.example.com in production, http://localhost:8000 in development

  A default would serve production with http:// links in the mails and
  a session cookie without Secure. In development, set it in the shell:
  BOARD_SITE_URL=http://localhost:8000 go run . (.env holds only the
  database's address). .env is not for the image (.dockerignore)
- The Dockerfile has two stages: build without cgo in the golang image
  (a static binary, no C library needed), then move the binary and an
  empty media/ to distroless's nonroot image, which has no shell.
  media/ is a volume, owned by that user (65532). Build from a checkout
  with the generated *_gen.go committed: the build needs no maago
  command
- This tutorial's go.mod uses the maago go.work points at. The image
  builds without go.work, so go get a released maago first

      docker build -t board .
      docker run --rm -e MAAGO_DATABASE_URL=postgres://… -e MAAGO_SUPERUSER_EMAIL=… -e MAAGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=… board -createsuperuser
      docker run -p 8000:8000 -e MAAGO_DATABASE_URL=postgres://… \
          -e BOARD_SITE_URL=https://board.example.com -v board-media:/srv/media board

  localhost in a container is not the host machine: write the
  database's address as the container reaches it

Before each release:

- Back the database up (pg_dump, or a snapshot of its volume). The
  binary migrates as it starts, so nobody looks at a migration between
  the build and the database
- There are no down migrations. A binary older than the database stops
  at the start with "the database is newer than this code": fix
  forward with a new release, or restore the backup. In a rolling
  release, an old copy that restarts stops the same way
- A long ALTER on PostgreSQL holds the table: the old copies' requests
  on it wait until it ends

On a platform:

- Give it more than 30s to stop: Docker's --stop-timeout (10s by
  default), Fly's kill_timeout (5s), Kubernetes'
  terminationGracePeriodSeconds (30s) all fall short of Serve's wait.
  For Kubernetes' preStop, distroless has no sleep binary: use the
  sleep action
- Health checks: the image has no curl, so no Dockerfile HEALTHCHECK.
  Use the platform's HTTP check on /app/ (it reads the database), with
  time to start: a copy waits for another's migration for up to 10
  minutes before it listens
- Behind a proxy, every request's r.RemoteAddr is the proxy, and the
  limit on sign-in attempts counts them all as one IP. Set RemoteAddr
  from the proxy's header in a middleware in main, for the proxy's own
  address alone (0021)

With SQLite instead: give the file an absolute path on a volume
(sqlite:/data/app.db; a relative one starts an empty database when the
working directory differs), in a directory owned by the image's user
(65532: made in the build stage and copied with --chown, as media/ is);
and
while a new copy migrates, the old one's writes wait 5 seconds and then
fail with a 500.

Two things to change in main before production: the mails still go to
the log (authlib.ConsoleMail); sending them is one line,
authlib.SMTP(addr, from, auth), and its settings. Config.Origins'
localhost:5173 is the dev server: put the production frontend's origin
there. When the proxy takes /app off, drop main's StripPrefix (0019).

